  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow on-navy reveal"><a href="../blog">Blog</a></span>
    <h1 class="display reveal" data-d="1">What does a reseller need from a new vendor before they will sell it?</h1>
    <p class="lead reveal" data-d="2">A reason their customers will want it, economics that work, protection for the deals they find and a vendor that is easy to work with. A good product alone is not enough.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<main id="main" tabindex="-1">
<!-- ARTICLE -->
<section class="section surface-page" id="article" style="padding-top:clamp(40px,6vw,80px)">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="post">
      <p class="post-byline">By <a href="../About">Taz Abbas</a>, Managing Partner<span aria-hidden="true"> · </span><span>Published <time datetime="2026-10-29">October 29, 2026</time></span><span aria-hidden="true"> · </span><a href="../partnerships">B2B Partnerships</a></p>
      <div class="prose">
<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>A reseller needs four things before it will put a new vendor in front of its customers: a reason its customers will want the product, a margin worth the effort, protection for the deals it finds, and a vendor that is easy to work with when something goes wrong. Most new vendors arrive with a good product and a price list. The ones that get sold arrive with all four.</p>

<h2 id="the-resellers-view">How a reseller looks at a new vendor</h2>
<p>A reseller's most limited resource is the attention of its sales team. Every new line card competes with products the team already knows how to sell, already has relationships for and already trusts to work. Adding a vendor means training, new paperwork, a new support path and the risk of a customer problem the reseller will have to own. So the first question is rarely whether the product is good. It is whether selling it is worth more than the next best use of that time.</p>
<p>That is why a strong product alone rarely gets a vendor sold. The reseller has to see a clear path from signing the agreement to closing a deal, and then to the work and revenue that follow.</p>

<h2 id="customer-demand">1. A reason customers will want it</h2>
<p>Resellers sell to customers they already have. Show them which of those customers need your product, what problem it solves for them and how it fits with what the reseller already sells. A product that completes an existing solution, or opens a conversation the reseller cannot have today, is far easier to adopt than one that stands alone.</p>
<p>Bring specifics: the type of customer, the trigger that starts a purchase, the people involved in the decision and the questions they ask. If you already have US customers, say which segments they come from. If you do not, say so plainly and explain why you expect demand. Resellers can tell when a vendor is guessing, and honesty here builds more trust than optimism.</p>

<h2 id="economics">2. Economics that work at every step</h2>
<p>Margin is the obvious part, but resellers look at the whole picture. They want to know the discount they buy at, whether it changes with volume or certification, what services they can sell around the product, and whether renewals and support contracts flow back to them. A product that leads to installation, integration, managed services or recurring renewals can be worth more to a reseller than one with a higher product margin and nothing after the sale.</p>
<p>Be clear about pricing to the end customer too. If you sell direct online at a price below what the reseller can offer, or if a distributor's other customers undercut them, the margin on paper does not matter. Resellers walk away from vendors whose pricing leaves them exposed.</p>

<h2 id="deal-protection">3. Protection for the deals they find</h2>
<p>A reseller that spends weeks developing an opportunity needs to know it will not lose that deal to another partner, or to the vendor's own sales team, at the last minute. Deal registration is the most common answer: the reseller registers the opportunity, and the vendor commits to supporting that partner on it, often with a better discount.</p>
<p>Whatever mechanism you choose, write it down, apply it consistently and respond to registrations quickly. A registration program that is slow or ignored when it matters does more harm than having none, because it teaches partners that your promises do not hold.</p>

<h2 id="easy-to-work-with">4. A vendor that is easy to work with</h2>
<p>This is where many new vendors fall short. Resellers want a named person who answers questions within a day, product training their team can complete without flying anywhere, sales materials they can put their own name on, demo access or evaluation units, and a clear path for technical escalations. They want to order through the distributor they already use if possible, with terms they already know.</p>
<p>Think about the first problem a reseller's customer will have. Who answers the phone? How fast? If the honest answer is "our team in another time zone, eventually," fix that before you recruit, or say clearly how support will work and what the reseller is expected to handle.</p>

<h2 id="what-to-prepare">What to have ready before you recruit</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A one-page summary of who the product is for, the problem it solves and how it fits with what resellers already sell.</li>
  <li>A partner price list with discounts, any volume or tier changes, and the recommended end-customer price.</li>
  <li>A written deal registration policy and the person who approves registrations.</li>
  <li>Short, practical training: what the product does, how to qualify a customer, how to demo it and how to handle the common objections.</li>
  <li>Co-brandable materials: a product sheet, a short presentation and a customer email the reseller can adapt.</li>
  <li>A support and escalation path with response times you can keep.</li>
  <li>An ordering route, ideally through a US distributor the reseller already buys from.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="first-ninety-days">Making the first months count</h2>
<p>Signing a reseller is the start, not the finish. Agree on a short list of target customers together, join the first few sales calls if the reseller wants you there, and celebrate the first deal in a way the reseller's team notices. Early wins are what turn a name on a partner list into a partner that sells. Keep the commitment small and specific on both sides, review it after a set period, and adjust the program based on what the first partners actually ask for.</p>

      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

